Hydra Engine is a fork of Ghidra (https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra), the
software reverse engineering framework developed by the National Security Agency. It is based on
Ghidra's `master` branch (12.3 DEV); the upstream commit each release is based on is named in
CHANGELOG.md, and upstream changes are merged in as they land. Ghidra's project format, decompiler,
analyzers, processor modules, loaders and scripting API are Ghidra's own, unchanged except as
listed below, and Hydra Engine depends on them. It is distributed under the same license as
Ghidra, the Apache License, Version 2.0 (see LICENSE). Ghidra's own notice is in NOTICE, and the
licenses of the third-party components Ghidra uses are in the licenses/ and GPL/ directories; all
of them apply to Hydra Engine unchanged.

This file is the notice of modifications required by section 4(b) of the Apache License,
Version 2.0. Release archives ship it next to NOTICE and LICENSE.


Files of upstream Ghidra that Hydra Engine modified or replaced
(every file below exists in upstream Ghidra; compared with the upstream commit named in
CHANGELOG.md):

  Ghidra/Framework/DB/src/main/java/db/buffers/LocalBufferFile.java
      A temporary buffer file is truncated to zero length before it is closed and deleted, so
      that ReFS (as on a Windows Dev Drive) does not write its unwritten pages back first.
      A comment at the top of the file says so.
  Ghidra/Framework/DB/src/main/java/db/buffers/LocalManagedBufferFile.java
      The header of a new version file is written and synced before the file is renamed to its
      final name, so a process killed during a save no longer leaves a final-named version file
      with an empty header. A comment at the top of the file says so.
  Ghidra/Framework/FileSystem/src/main/java/ghidra/framework/store/db/VersionedDatabase.java
      The newest version file is checked when a database is opened (see VersionFileCheck below).
      A comment at the top of the file says so.
  Ghidra/Framework/FileSystem/src/main/java/ghidra/framework/store/local/LocalDatabaseItem.java
      A new private database item gets its file type only once its content is complete, so an
      item whose writer was killed is removed as incomplete instead of being listed as a program
      that cannot be opened. A comment at the top of the file says so.
      The newest version is also checked when an item is opened; an unreadable newest version of
      a disposable, writable item is quarantined and the previous version is used, and an item
      opened read-only is never changed (answers corrupt_newest).
  Ghidra/Framework/SoftwareModeling/src/main/java/ghidra/app/plugin/processors/sleigh/SleighLanguageFile.java
      The language lock file is created the first time a language needs it instead of for every
      language description at start-up. A comment at the top of the file says so.
  gradle/distributableGhidraModule.gradle
      Two lines: a module's support/ folder is copied into the distribution without
      __pycache__ folders and .pyc files.
  build.gradle
      One line at the end: `apply from: 'gradle/hydra/headless.gradle'`.
  .gitattributes
      Three lines: scripts in .github/scripts keep LF line endings.
  README.md
      Replaced by Hydra Engine's README. Ghidra's original README is kept, byte for byte, as
      README-GHIDRA.md (a new file).
  .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/bug_report.md, feature_request.md, question.md
      Removed; replaced by issue forms (bug_report.yml, feature_request.yml, config.yml).

Every other file of upstream Ghidra, including its .github/workflows, is as published by its
authors.


What Hydra Engine adds to Ghidra (new files, none of them in upstream Ghidra):
  - the HeadlessSession module (Ghidra/Features/HeadlessSession): a shared headless session
    daemon, its Python launcher and client, and their tests;
  - a headless-only build (gradle/hydra/headless.gradle, the buildHeadless task);
  - Ghidra/Framework/FileSystem/src/main/java/ghidra/framework/store/db/VersionFileCheck.java, one
    added class in a Ghidra package (header validation and the quarantine of an unreadable newest
    version file);
  - the repository's own documentation, assets and CI (NOTICE-HYDRA, CHANGELOG.md,
    README-GHIDRA.md, and the files in .github/: CONTRIBUTING.md, SECURITY.md, issue forms, the
    pull request template, assets, scripts, and the ci.yml and release.yml workflows).


